The attached image (Fig. 3) illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site and is taken up by recruited cells. Panel A, a line graph, shows the kinetics of various immune cell populations, revealing that Mac-2+ macrophages and Ly6C+ monocytes are the predominant inflammatory cells, peaking around day 2, while neutrophils are recruited early but decline rapidly. Panel B, composed of confocal microscopy images, visually confirms the intracellular presence of fluorescently labeled Nano-11 (red) within MHCII+ antigen-presenting cells, Ly6C+ monocytes, and Mac-2+ macrophages, indicating efficient cellular uptake and retention at the injection site over several days.